From 71f8cd1428888ef30ff81e833e5065b3da7235b5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jawad Amin <jawadamin@google.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 07:53:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/8] Give every V4.1 sliding-window layer its own KV cache
 array

V4 lets a sliding-window layer share a cache array with a compressed
NoPE layer. V4.1 cannot. Its sparse attention kernel reads the NoPE
array outside the block table on purpose: the kernel gives every
unfilled top-k slot a pseudo-random page and offset, so a padded gather
spreads across the array instead of hitting one row. Those reads can
land on any row, so the array must hold NoPE records and nothing else.

A sliding-window row is BF16. Its bytes decode as an E8M0 scale of
2 ** (byte - 127), which reaches 2e33, and the byte 0xFF decodes as a
nan. The kernel comment at sparse_mla.py already names this hazard.

Add two instruments as well:

- The attention layer prints a nan flag, an inf flag and a magnitude for
  each intermediate tensor when DSV41_TRACE_LAYER_STATS is 1.
- The model reports every declared parameter that no checkpoint tensor
  filled. The vLLM loader checks the checkpoint side only, so a
  parameter the checkpoint never names keeps the arbitrary bit pattern
  that torch.empty returned.

Cost: four more cache arrays, about 283 MiB per chip on a v6e-16.
